sustainable, and clean energy sources, and new technologies related to energy storage, conversion, and detection [1]. Therein, supercapacitors, with high power density, fast charge and discharge rates, and good cyclic stability, have become the object of vigorous development [2,3]. However, the low capacitance and energy density of supercapacitors make them limited in their applications [4,5]. Thus, the discovery of effective electrode materials is central to the development of electrochemistry, not only for supercapacitors, but also for water splitting. Meanwhile, the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) is the key electrochemical reactions in energy conversion. However, the slow kinetics and high overpotential lead to low productivity efficiency in the process of OER [6,7]. In addition, electrochemical sensing is a potential monitoring method which has the advantages of fast response, high sensitivity, and low cost [8,9]. Similarly, their performances are closely dependent on the properties of electrode materials. In general, the design of a new electrode material is the key to the next generation of electrochemical technology.

MOF is a metal organic framework material formed by the combination of metal ions or clusters and organic ligands, which exhibits large specific surface area, adjustable structure, and highly exposed active sites to be applied to gas separation [10], sensing [11,12], drug carrier [13], catalysis [14,15], and other fields [16,17]. So far, people have been working to develop multifunctional MOF. For example, Qi et al. designed the bifunctional electrocatalyst NiFe-MOF by regulating organic ligands to increase their unsaturated metal sites which can accelerate the electrocatalytic activity, the adsorption of \(-\mathrm{OH}\) and \(-\mathrm{OH}^{-}\), and enlarge the pore size and the specific surface area, so it has excellent performance in both the oxygen evolution reaction and the hydrogen evolution reaction [18]. Although the versatility of MOF materials has been rapidly developed in recent years, there are few reports involving energy storage, catalysis, and sensing based on the same materials. However, the low conductivity of MOF always hinders its development in these applications [19,20]. To solve this problem, one method is to design MOF with high conductivity, such as the introduction of metal/ligand defects, heterometallic ions, and the development of conjugated organic ligands etc [21]. For example, conductive metal MOF \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HTIP})_{2}\) is constructed by metal ion and conjugated organic ligands to improve supercapacitor performance [22]. However, in many cases, the design of pristine MOFs cannot provide an ideal performance to satisfy practical applications. Another method is to introduce conductive agents into MOF. Various conductive materials, including graphene, polyaniline, metal oxides and metal hydroxides, etc [23–25], can be coupled with MOFs to enhance multifunctional applications. Liu et al. designed a material in which Fe-MOF synergised with RGO greatly improved the electrochemical energy storage performance [26]. Meanwhile, \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) as another promising material that has attracted widespread attention due to its low cost, environmental friendliness, and excellent specific capacitance [27,28]. Zhao et al. used \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{SiO}_{4}\) nanobelt as a template to coat \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) to prepare \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{SiO}_{4}@\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) core–shell heterostructure, in which the electrochemical performance is greatly pronounced in contrast to the original material [29]. In the \(\mathrm{CoMnFe}@\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) electrode material prepared by Guo et al., the growth of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) makes the material have highly exposed active centres, rapid ion and electron transport, and gas release channels, which makes it exhibit an excellent electrocatalytic performance [30]. Huang et al. prepared the \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}@\mathrm{3DPN}\) electrode by growing cross-linked \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) nanosheets on a 3D porous nickel template, which showed high sensitivity and fast response in the electrochemical detection of glucose [31]. The results show that the composite material can significantly enhance the electrochemical performance. Inspired by the above research, by combining the advantages of MOFs and Ni

\(\left(0\mathrm{H}\right)_{2}\) to construct heterostructure composite materials, the electrochemical performance of MOFs is greatly improved.

In this paper, we propose a feasible scheme of constructing Ni \(\left(0\mathrm{H}\right)_{2}\) into MOF; the hybrid not only presents enhanced conductivity through the combination of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) to optimise the electronic structure and improve the electron interactions between both, but also retains a series of advantages of MOF, such as porous structure and flexible tunability, both of which have a significant improvement in the performance of energy storage, electrocatalytic, and electrical sensing.

# 2. Experimental section

# 2.1. Chemical reagent

Iron (III) nitrate nonahydrate, terephthalic acid, N, N-Dimethylformamide (DMF), \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\), Nickel sulfate, Potassium peroxydisulfate, \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}0\) (25–28%), Bisphenol A, Polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF), 1-Methyl-2-pyrrolidinone (NMP) are purchased by Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. All chemicals are used without further purification.

# 2.2. Preparation of Fe-MOF

The preparation of Fe-MOF is according to a previous method reported in the literature [32]. \(0.81\ \mathrm{~g~}\) of \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}\cdot9\mathrm{H}_{2}0\) and \(0.33\mathrm{~g~}\) of terephthalic acid are dissolved by \(40~\mathrm{mL}\) mixed solution of DMF and \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) \((\mathbf{v}:v=1:1)\), then the above solution is added into a \(50~\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined autoclave at \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(40\,\mathrm{\min}\). The obtained products are collected by centrifugation, washed by DMF and ethanol several times, and dried at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).

# 2.3. Synthesis of Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)2

A \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) mixed solution is prepared consisting of \(5~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(1\mathrm{~M~}\) nickel sulphate, \(4~\mathrm{mL}\) or \(0.25~\mathrm{M}\) potassium persulphate, and \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) ammonia \((25{-}28\%)\). Subsequently, the Fe-MOF powder \(_{(0.5\mathrm{~g)}}\) is added into the mixed solution and allowed to stand for \(0\,\mathrm{\min}\), \(10\,\mathrm{min}\), \(20\,\mathrm{min}\), \(30\,\mathrm{min}\). The powder is concentrated by centrifugation, washed several times with deionised water, and dried in a vacuum at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The products are named Fe-MOF and Fe\(\mathrm{MOF@Ni(OH)}_{2}-\mathrm{X}\), where \(\mathrm{X}=10\), 20, 30.

# 2.4. Characterization

Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) is applied to observed the morphology and structure (Gemini SEM 300). Energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) is applied to parse the elemental composition of substances. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) investigates the internal structure of samples. The crystal structure of the material is studied by X-ray diffraction (XRD, Bruker, D8 Advance). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analyze the composition and valence of compounds. The nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherm at \(77\,\mathrm{~k~}\) obtains specific surface area and pore diameter distribution. Fourier infrared spectroscopy mastered the functional group (FTIR Thermo Nicolet, NEXUS 670). TGA is performed on a thermogravimetric analyzer (STA449 F5) under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) flow between 25 and \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with \(10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}\).

# 2.5. Electrochemical measurements

All electrochemical measurements are proceeded with a CHI 660E electrochemical workstation at room temperature. Fe-MOF and \(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)}_{2}-\mathrm{X}\) are used as the working electrode, \(\mathrm{Hg}/\)
HgO is the reference electrode, and the platinum plate is the counter electrode. Cyclic voltammetry (CV), galvanostatic charge–discharge (GCD), and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS, frequency range \(0.01{-}100{,}000\ \mathrm{Hz}\)) measurements are performed using a standard three-electrode configuration in 3 M KOH alkaline electrolyte. The calculation formula of specific capacity: \(C_{m}=\mathrm{I}\Delta\mathrm{t}/(\mathrm{m}\Delta\mathrm{V})\), where I is current density \((\mathsf{A}\,\,\mathsf{g}^{-1})\); \(\Delta{\sf t}\) is discharge time (s); \(\mathbf{m}\) is the mass of active material (g); \(\Delta\mathsf{V}\) is working potential (v).

For electrochemical measurement of asymmetric supercapacitors, Nickel foam is ultrasonic treated with 3 M HCl, ethanol and water and dried prior to treatment of the electrode material. After mixing the active material, PVDF, and conductive carbon black at a mass ratio of 8: 1: 1 uniformly, the paste is coated on the pretreated foamed nickel. The coating area is \(1\,\times\,1\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) and dried to prepare an electrode, the load is about \(3\mathrm{~mg~cm}^{-2}\). Activated carbon is served as the negative electrode material, and the positive electrode is composite material. The two are assembled into an asymmetric supercapacitor with 3 M KOH electrolyte. The energy density is calculated \(\mathrm{E}=1/2\,\mathrm{CV}^{2}\). The calculation formula of power density is \(\mathrm{P}=1/2\,\mathrm{CV}^{2}/\Delta\mathrm{t}\), where C is specific capacity as \(\mathsf{F}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), V is Voltage window (v), \(\Delta{\sf t}\) is discharge time as s.

To the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) tests, we use prepared samples serving as the working electrode, platinum sheet serving as the counter electrode, and a standard \(\mathrm{Hg/HgO}\) electrode serving as the reference. \(2\;\mathrm{mg}\) of catalyst and \(10\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(5\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) Nafion are dispersed in \(470~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) ethanol and then sonicated for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) to form a homogeneous ink. \(5~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of catalyst ink is dropped onto the glassy carbon electrode. Carbon paper is used in stability tests. The linear sweep voltammograms (LSV) curves are noted in \(1\,\mathrm{~M~}\,\mathrm{KOH}\) at a scan rate of \(5\:\mathrm{\mV\s^{-1}}\). The Tafel slope are built as: \(\eta\;=\;b l o g\) \((j)=a,\eta,\,a,b\) and \(j\) are represented as the overpotential. Tafel constant, the Tafel slope and the current density, respectively. They are derived from recorded LSV curves. The potential relative to RHE can be calculated using formula: E \((\mathrm{RHE})=\mathrm{E}\) \((\mathrm{Hg}/\mathrm{HgO})+0.098\,+\) \(0.059\mathrm{pH}\).

For BPA detection, Saturated calomel is the reference electrode, and the platinum sheet is the counter electrode, the material is dropped on the glassy carbon electrode as the working electrode according to the above method. The electrolyte is \(50\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\,\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) BPA in \(0.01\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) phosphate buffer solution \((\mathsf{p H}\,7.4)\). Differential pulse voltammetry (DPV) measurement and CV measurement are performed in the potential range of \(0{-}0.8\,\mathrm{V}\).

# 3. Result and discussion

# 3.1. Characterisation

The \(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)_{2}}\) composite was prepared via a two-step process including the hydrothermal and physical deposition method, as schematically illustrated in Fig. 1a. Firstly, Fe-MOF was prepared by solvothermal synthesis in a mixed solution of \(\mathsf{F e}^{3+}\) and terephthalic acid. As can be seen from the SEM image shown in Fig. 1b, Fe-MOF possesses a regular spindle morphology with a length of \(300{-}400\ \mathrm{nm}\). Secondly, a layer of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) species is uniformly grown on the surface of Fe-MOF \((\mathrm{Fe-MOF}@\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}{-}\mathrm{X})\) by adjusting the time of the physical deposition [33].

Compared with Fe-MOF, the morphology of \(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)_{2}{-}X}\) changed greatly, as shown in Fig. 1c, d and Figure S1. With the increase of the deposition time, a large amount of nanosheets appeared on the surface of the Fe-MOF, which are vertically and evenly distributed on the spindle skeleton and connected to form a 3D network structure with each other, indicating the preliminary synthesis of \(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)_{2}.X}\). Meanwhile, the prolonged deposition leads to the stacking of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) nanosheets and blockage

of pores, as shown in the Figure S1c. In addition, the elemental mapping images demonstrate that Fe, Ni, C, and O elements are uniformly distributed in the \(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)_{2}-20}\) (Fig. 1e–i). It is further confirmed that the \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) is uniformly deposited on the surface of Fe-MOF. Subsequently, the thermal stability of Fe-MOF is determined by the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), as shown in Figure S2. The TGA results show that Fe-MOF loses \(30\%\) mass before \(280\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), mainly due to the loss of crystal water and a molecule of DMF. During the process between \(280\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(460\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), DMF is lost in the pore structure. When the temperature is between \(600\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(630\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the compound begins to decompose, and the skeleton structure is destroyed. The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns for Fe-MOF and Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)2-X are shown in Fig. 1i. The XRD spectrum of Fe-MOF can be indexed to the simulated XRD spectrum of MIL-88B, which proves that the prepared sample is MIL-88B. For the composite materials, except for the characteristic diffraction peaks of Fe-MOF, all the characteristic peaks are matched with \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) (JCPDS No. 38-0715), and the sharp and clear characteristic diffraction peak (star pattern) angles are \(11.3^{\circ}\), \(22.7^{\circ}\), \(34.4^{\circ}\), and \(60^{\circ}\), belonging to the (0 0 3), (0 0 6), (0 1 2), and (1 1 0) crystal planes of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\), respectively (Fig. 1j). This result, which shows the coexistence of the Fe-MOF and \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}{-}\mathrm{X}\), indicates that Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)2-X is successfully prepared.

The morphology and nanostructures were further investigated by transmission electron microscopy (TEM). Fe-MOF (part of the red coil) is covered by a large number of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) nanosheets (part of the blue coil), as shown in Fig. 2a. This result is consistent with the SEM. The high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) images indicate that the \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) is crystalline (Fig. 2b, c), the observed crystal fringes of \(0.232\,\mathrm{~nm}\) and \(0.190\,\mathrm{~nm}\) are mainly attributed to the (0 1 8) and (0 1 2) lattice planes of Ni \((\mathrm{OH})_{2}\), respectively. However, the selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern (inset in Fig. 2b, c) obtained by the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT), have a weak diffraction ring to prove that the material shows poor crystallinity.

X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was used to further characterise the electronic valence state and chemical composition of \(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)_{2}-20}\) composites. The coexisting elements are identified by XPS full survey spectrum as C, O, Ni and Fe (Fig. 3a), which is consistent with the results of the EDS mapping analysis. It also indicates that \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) nanosheets are grown on the Fe-MOF surface. The C1s of \(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)_{2}-20}\) is deconvoluted to different peaks at binding energies of \(284.8\,\mathrm{~eV}\), \(286.4\,\mathrm{~eV}\), and \(288.5\,\mathrm{~eV}\), which are assigned to the C–C, C–O, and O–C=O bonds [34,35] (Fig. 3b). The C1s spectra displays one peak at a binding energy of \(531.2\,\mathrm{~eV}\) corresponding to \(\mathrm{O-H}\), which further testifies to the existence of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) [36,37] (Fig. 3c). The XPS spectrum of Ni 2p clearly shows two distinct peaks at the binding energies of 873.6 and \(856.0\,\mathrm{~eV}\), which are severally assigned to Ni \(2{\bf p}_{1/2}\) and Ni \(2{\bf p}_{3/2}\). In addition, there are two satellite peaks at 879.8 and \(861.0\,\mathrm{~eV}\) in Fig. 3d, which are in line with the feature of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) in \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) [38]. Where the appearance of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) is in a higher oxidation state, surface oxidation may occur [39]. The high-resolution Fe 2p XPS spectra of \(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)_{2}-20}\) is shown in Fig. 3e. The peaks at \(712.4\,\mathrm{~eV}\) and \(725.3\,\mathrm{~eV}\) are in accordance with Fe \(2{\bf p}_{3/2}\) and \(2{\bf p}_{1/2}\), and can be attributed to \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\), manifesting that \(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)_{2}-20}\) only contains \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) without \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\). The presence of \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) may induce changes in the electronic structure of \(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)_{2}}\) due to the strong coordination interaction between \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) and carboxylic oxygen, which will improve the strength of the interaction with the OER intermediate and affect its electrochemical performance. Based on the above XPS results and Figure S3, after growing a layer of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) on the surface of Fe-MOF, the binding energy of Fe \(2{\bf p}_{3/2}\) has shifted positively by \(0.5\,\mathrm{~eV}\) after growing a layer of Ni \((\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) on the surface of Fe-MOF, while compared to \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\), the \(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)_{2}-20}\) on \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{P}_{1/2}\) peak has shifted to a lower bind
