The adsorption–desorption isotherm and pore size distribution curve (Figure S4) were analysed to acquire the BET specific surface area and pore size distribution of Fe-MOF and Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)2– 20. \(248.640~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) is the specific area of Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)2–0, and the specific surface area of Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)2–20 is 34.313 \(\mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) . The main pore size distributions of the two are \(30\ \mathrm{nm}\) and \(28~\mathrm{nm}\) . It is noted that the specific surface area and pore size of Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)2–20 are less than that of Fe-MOF, which is due to the introduction of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) . Therefore, the Fe-MOF surface has been successfully covered by \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) nanosheets.

# 3.2. Electrochemical measurement

The electrochemical performances of Fe-MOF and Fe-MOF@Ni \((0\mathrm{H})_{2}{-}\mathrm{X}\) were evaluated on a three-electrode setup (Fig. 4 and Fig

ure S5). Fig. 4a shows the CV curves comparison of different electrodes at a scan rate of \(5\,\mathrm{\mV\s^{-1}}\) . The CV curve area of Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)2–20 is larger than that of the other samples, indicating that it provided superior capacitive behaviour. Moreover, with the increase of scan rate, the CV curves of all the electrodes remain in a similar shape with less distortion, indicating good capacitive behaviour and a high rate capability, as shown in Fig. 4c and Figure S5. The GCD curves of Fe-MOF and Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)2-X are compared in Fig. 4b. It can be seen that the capability of Fe-MOF is greatly improved through physical deposition treatment. Therein, the Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)2–20 has the longest discharge time, indicating that the maximum capacity can be stored, which is consistent with the CV test results. Fig. 4c shows the CV curves of Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)2–20 at a potential of \(0{-}0.7\,\mathrm{V}\) at different scan rates. The peak current and area of the CV curves with sweep rate at 5 to \(80\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) gradually accelerate, and there is a pair of obvious redox peaks, indicating that the energy storage of the materials mostly originates from the faradaic pseudo-capacitance. Meanwhile, the GCD curves of Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)2–20 at different current densities have been studied. It can be seen that with the increase of current density, the discharge time of the electrode material decreases gradually, indicating that the mass specific capacity of the material decreases gradually. This situation occurs when the electrode material is charged and discharged at a high current density. The charging and discharging speed is too fast, leading to insufficient electrolyte ions penetration into the electrode material, resulting in an insufficient electrochemical reaction and large voltage drop (Fig. 4d). The calculated specific capacities of the Fe-MOF and Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)2-X electrodes as a function of scan rate and based on the GCD curves, are shown in Fig. 4e. Notably, the Fe-MOF@Ni \((0\mathrm{H})_{2}{-}20\) yields a remarkable specific capacity of \(168\;\mathrm{mAh}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\) , which is much larger than \(47.5\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}\) for the Fe-MOF, \(59.1\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for the Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)2–10, and \(87.5\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}\) for Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)2–30 at the same current density. Fig. 4f is the Nyquist plot with amplitudes of \(5\;\mathrm{mV}\) at frequencies ranging from \(10^{6}\,\mathrm{Hz}\) to \(0.01\;\mathrm{Hz}\) . The impedance diagram of these electrodes can
