The electrochemical behaviour of \(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)_{2}}{-}20\) was further analysed by fitting its CV curves (Fig. 5a) according to the following formula[43]:

\[
\mathrm{Log}i=\mathrm{blog}\nu+\mathrm{log}a
\]

where \(i\) is peak current value, \(\nu\) is sweep speed, and a and b are constants. \(b\) is connected with charge storage mechanism: (1) if \(\mathbf{b}=0.5\) , Fe-MOF@Ni \(\mathrm{OH})_{2}{-}20\) shows a battery behaviour; (2) \(\mathbf{b}=0.5{-}1\) , Fe\(\mathrm{MOF@Ni(OH)_{2}}{-}20\) shows the features of battery and pseudocapacitance; (3) \(\textbf{b}\geq\textbf{1}\) , Fe-MOF@Ni \(\mathrm{OH})_{2}{-}20\) exhibits pseudocapacitance properties. According to the formula calculation, the b value of \(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)_{2}}{-}20\) under different peak currents is

0.44 and 0.50, suggesting that the charge and discharge process is mainly diffusion controlled. Furthermore, the contribution rate of \(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)_{2}}{-}20\) to diffusion control and the pseudocapacitance control to charge storage at different scan rates has also been studied. The contribution rate is calculated as follows [44,45]:

\[
\mathrm{I}(\mathrm{V})/\mathrm{v}^2=k_1\mathrm{v}_1^1+\mathrm{k}_2
\]

where \(\nu\) is the sweep speed of a specific voltage, \(I\) is the current at a specific voltage, and \(k_{1}\) and \(k_{2}\) are constants. \(k_{1}\nu\) is the dedication of pseudo-capacitance to current. It can be seen from the CV curve (Fig. 5b) and histogram (Fig. 5c) that the contributions of diffusion control to capacitance from \(5\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) to \(80\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) are \(98\%\) , \(97.5\%\) , \(96.5\%\) , \(94.5\%\) , and \(92.5\%\) , respectively (Fig. 5c). This test result shows that the \(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@Ni(OH)_{2}}{-}20\) charge storage is mainly carried out through the diffusion process.

